To perform division in complex numbers, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. In this case, the conjugate of $1+2i$ is $1-2i$. Multiplying: \begin{align*}
\frac{-3+4i}{1+2i}&=\frac{(-3+4i)(1-2i)}{(1+2i)(1-2i)}\\
&=\frac{-3+4i+6i-8i^2}{1+2i-2i-4i^2}\\
&=\frac{5+10i}{5}\\
&=\boxed{1+2i}
\end{align*}

Note that $AD = 6,$ $BD = 4,$ and $CD = 2.$  Then by Pythagoras, $AB = 2 \sqrt{13},$ $AC = 2 \sqrt{10},$ and $BC = 2 \sqrt{5}.$  By Heron's Theorem,
\begin{align*}
[ABC]^2 &= (\sqrt{5} + \sqrt{10} + \sqrt{13})(-\sqrt{5} + \sqrt{10} + \sqrt{13})(\sqrt{5} - \sqrt{10} + \sqrt{13})(\sqrt{5} + \sqrt{10} - \sqrt{13}) \\
&= ((\sqrt{10} + \sqrt{13})^2 - (\sqrt{5})^2)((\sqrt{5})^2 - (\sqrt{10} - \sqrt{13})^2) \\
&= (18 + 2 \sqrt{130})(2 \sqrt{130} - 18) \\
&= 196,
\end{align*}so $[ABC] = 14.$

